### What is the Proxy pattern?

The Proxy pattern provides a surrogate or placeholder for another object to control access to it. The proxy implements the same interface as the real object, so the client cannot tell the difference, but the proxy can add behaviour before or after delegating.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface AccountService {
    BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId);
    void withdraw(String accountId, BigDecimal amount);
}
```

### Why does the Proxy pattern exist?

Without a proxy, adding concerns like security, caching, or lazy initialisation means modifying the real class, which violates the Single Responsibility Principle. The proxy lets you layer concerns around the real object without touching its code.

In banking, Rajesh needs a balance check to be cached for performance, access-controlled for security, and logged for audit. Without a proxy, all three concerns would be tangled inside the real `AccountService`.

```java
// Without proxy: tangled concerns in the real class (bad)
public class AccountServiceImpl implements AccountService {
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        checkPermission();  // security concern
        if (cache.has(accountId)) return cache.get(accountId); // caching concern
        log("getBalance called"); // audit concern
        // actual business logic buried under cross-cutting concerns
    }
}
```

### What are the types of Proxy?

There are five standard types, each adding a different concern.

#### Virtual proxy (lazy initialisation)

Defers creating an expensive real object until it is first actually needed. The proxy holds a null reference and creates the real object on the first method call.

```java
package com.bank.proxy;

import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import com.bank.service.AccountServiceImpl;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LazyAccountProxy implements AccountService {
    private AccountService real;

    private AccountService getReal() {
        if (real == null) {
            real = new AccountServiceImpl(); // expensive creation deferred
        }
        return real;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return getReal().getBalance(accountId);
    }

    @Override
    public void withdraw(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        getReal().withdraw(accountId, amount);
    }
}
```

#### Protection proxy (access control)

Enforces authorisation before delegating. In banking, only an authorised teller or the account owner can call `withdraw`.

```java
package com.bank.proxy;

import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SecureAccountProxy implements AccountService {
    private final AccountService real;
    private final String currentUserRole;

    public SecureAccountProxy(AccountService real, String currentUserRole) {
        this.real = real;
        this.currentUserRole = currentUserRole;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return real.getBalance(accountId); // read is allowed for all
    }

    @Override
    public void withdraw(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        if (!"TELLER".equals(currentUserRole)
                && !"MANAGER".equals(currentUserRole)) {
            throw new SecurityException(
                "Role " + currentUserRole + " cannot withdraw");
        }
        real.withdraw(accountId, amount);
    }
}
```

#### Caching proxy

Remember the results of expensive operations and returns the cached value on repeat calls.

```java
package com.bank.proxy;

import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class CachingAccountProxy implements AccountService {
    private final AccountService real;
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public CachingAccountProxy(AccountService real) {
        this.real = real;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return cache.computeIfAbsent(accountId, real::getBalance);
    }

    @Override
    public void withdraw(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        real.withdraw(accountId, amount);
        cache.remove(accountId); // invalidate stale entry
    }
}
```

#### Remote proxy

Represents an object that lives in another address space, hiding network communication. Java RMI stubs are remote proxies.

#### Smart proxy

Adds extra actions like logging, reference counting, or lock management around access.

### How do JDK dynamic proxies work?

A JDK dynamic proxy is generated at runtime using `java.lang.reflect.Proxy.newProxyInstance`. It requires the target to implement at least one interface and routes every method call through an `InvocationHandler`.

```java
package com.bank.proxy;

import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import com.bank.service.AccountServiceImpl;
import java.lang.reflect.InvocationHandler;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class LoggingProxyFactory {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger("AuditProxy");

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static <T> T create(T target, Class<T> iface) {
        return (T) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
            iface.getClassLoader(),
            new Class[]{iface},
            (proxy, method, args) -> {
                log.info("Before: " + method.getName());
                Object result = method.invoke(target, args);
                log.info("After: " + method.getName());
                return result;
            }
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountService real = new AccountServiceImpl();
        AccountService proxied = create(real, AccountService.class);
        proxied.getBalance("SAV2001");
    }
}
```

```text
Before: getBalance
After: getBalance
```

### How does CGLIB differ from JDK proxies?

CGLIB generates a runtime subclass of the target class, so it works on concrete classes without interfaces. However, it cannot proxy `final` classes or `final` methods because subclassing is impossible for them.

| Aspect | JDK Dynamic Proxy | CGLIB |
|---|---|---|
| Requirement | Target must implement an interface | No interface needed |
| Mechanism | Implements interface at runtime | Generates subclass at runtime |
| Final classes | Works (proxies the interface) | Cannot proxy final classes |
| Final methods | N/A (interface methods) | Cannot override final methods |
| Performance | Slightly faster for interface calls | Slightly slower due to subclassing |
| Used by | Spring default when interface exists | Spring fallback when no interface |

### How does Spring AOP use dynamic proxies?

When you annotate a method with `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable`, or `@Async`, Spring does not modify your class. At startup, Spring wraps your bean in a proxy.

- If your bean implements an interface, Spring creates a JDK dynamic proxy.
- If your bean has no interface, Spring uses CGLIB to create a subclass proxy.

When another bean calls your method, it really calls the proxy. The proxy runs the advice around your method.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    @Transactional
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Spring proxy opens a transaction BEFORE this method
        accountRepo.debit(fromId, amount);
        accountRepo.credit(toId, amount);
        // Spring proxy commits on success, rolls back on exception
    }
}
```

### What is the self-invocation problem?

Because the proxy wraps the bean from outside, a call from one method of a bean to another method of the same bean using `this.otherMethod()` bypasses the proxy entirely. Annotations like `@Transactional` on the inner method silently do nothing.

```java
@Service
public class LoanService {

    public void processLoan(String loanId) {
        // This call bypasses the proxy, so @Transactional does NOT apply
        this.approveLoan(loanId);
    }

    @Transactional
    public void approveLoan(String loanId) {
        // Transaction is NOT active because the proxy was bypassed
    }
}
```

The fixes are to call the annotated method from another bean, to inject the bean into itself, or to use `AopContext.currentProxy()`.

### What are common mistakes with the Proxy pattern?

The first mistake is not understanding that JDK proxies require interfaces. If you remove the interface from a bean, Spring silently switches to CGLIB, which can break if the class is final.

```java
// Wrong: final class cannot be proxied by CGLIB
@Service
public final class AccountService { // Spring cannot create a CGLIB proxy
    @Transactional
    public void debit(String id, BigDecimal amount) { }
}
```

The second mistake is confusing Proxy with Decorator. Both wrap an object, but their intent differs: Proxy controls access, Decorator adds behaviour.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer understands that most Spring "magic" is just the Proxy pattern. `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable`, `@Async`, and `@Secured` all work by wrapping beans in proxies. Understanding this explains why self-invocation breaks annotations, why final classes cause errors, and why proxied methods must be public.

In production at National Bank of India, Pankaj wraps the account service in a dynamic proxy that adds a short-lived balance cache and a role-based access check, so repeated balance lookups are served from cache and only a teller can withdraw, all without editing the real service.

```java
// Composing proxies: cache + security + real service
AccountService real = new AccountServiceImpl();
AccountService cached = new CachingAccountProxy(real);
AccountService secured = new SecureAccountProxy(cached, "TELLER");
secured.withdraw("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));
```
